  "scope": "Exactly this edit in test_run_migrations.py: in test_the_migration_creates_the_tables_and_reruns_as_a_no_op, `assert applied == 1` becomes `assert applied == len(shipped)`, where `shipped` lists the migration files in src/screamingface_engine/runs/migrations. And in packages/screamingface/tests/e2e/harness/stack.py and inspect_stack.py: one added entry in the Engine child's environment, `URL4_CLOUD_DATABASE_URL` set to an `engine.sqlite3` in the stack's own work dir, with its comment. Also (2026-11-09): Exactly the stand-ins of the job runner and the run queue in these files: each `schedule()` or `publish()` stand-in gains an `enqueued_at` keyword parameter (default None) that it accepts and ignores, plus the typing import it needs (test_queue_metrics.py, _fakes.py). In test_queue_admission.py the inline `refresh_then_fail` publish stand-in gains the same parameter. No assertion, fixture value or behaviour changes.",
  "reason": "E5 P1 spec D12 (PR #1648 review): a second Run registry migration, 0002_candidate_stop_owed, ships in this PR, so the applied count is the number of migrations that ship, not 1. The harness entries: a local Engine holds its Run database exclusively, so concurrent E2E stacks sharing the default `~/.screamingface-engine/engine.sqlite3` refused to start; each stack now has its own file. Also (2026-11-09): E5 P1 spec D12 (PR #1648 review): a bound start's run deadline counts from its bind, so the job runner's `schedule()` and the run queue's `publish()` gained an optional `enqueued_at`. Every stand-in of those two methods must accept the new keyword or the production call raises TypeError; nothing else in these tests changes.",
